<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1052</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad was the Uqaylid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and the most important ruler of the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a>, reigning from 1001 to about 1050. He held northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, briefly recognising the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 1010 before returning to Abbasid allegiance. He was a patron of poets, and in his last years he was deposed by his brother Baraka and died in captivity in 1052.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qirwash-ibn-al-muqallad.jpg" alt="Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27tamid_al-Dawla_Qirwash_1001-1050_AR_Dirham_Awana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
